<%= link_to "&laquo; #{@game.name}".html_safe, game_path(@game), class: "back-link" %>

<h1>Military</h1>
<p class="subtitle">
  What each army was made of, sampled every
  <%= ArmyCompositionsController::SAMPLE_INTERVAL %> turns and at the last turn recorded.
</p>

<% if @histories.empty? %>
  <p class="empty-state">No military data in this game's snapshots.</p>
<% else %>
  <% @histories.each do |history| %>
    <h2><%= history[:civ] %></h2>

    <table class="army">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Turn</th>
          <th>Units</th>
          <th>Might</th>
          <th>Power</th>
          <th>Power per unit</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <% history[:series].each do |entry| %>
          <tr>
            <td><%= entry[:turn] %></td>
            <td><%= entry[:units] || "&mdash;".html_safe %></td>
            <td><%= entry[:might] || "&mdash;".html_safe %></td>
            <td><%= entry[:army_power] || "&mdash;".html_safe %></td>
            <td><%= entry[:power_per_unit] || "&mdash;".html_safe %></td>
          </tr>
        <% end %>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  <% end %>

  <p class="note">
    Might is the figure the game reports, and it is not the strength of
    the army alone: it is the summed unit power multiplied by the
    treasury, up to double, so a civilization hoarding gold reads as
    fielding better units than it does. Power divides that multiplier
    back out. Naval units still count half, so an obsolete fleet drags
    the average down twice over.
  </p>
  <p class="note">
    Power per unit says what an army is made of, not how strong it is: a
    horde of obsolete units and a small modern force can carry the same
    total. The figure climbs for everyone as better units become
    available, so it is worth comparing between civilizations at the same
    turn rather than along one civilization's own history. Losing a war
    can raise it, because the weakest units die first.
  </p>
<% end %>
